{"id":"44cecf471a8cf8b6351cfeb67efbfdb9905a3459d854d9bbd2c5b873ece1b768","lamport":3,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-11-08T21:41:49.486127652Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"spec":"Running `ti sync` in a clone that has unsynced local work can lose that work and bring back tickets the user deleted. After one sync, comments written locally vanished, sub-issue links and dependencies added locally disappeared, and two tickets deleted locally reappeared. The sync reported success. There was no way to repair a parent/child link from the CLI afterwards: `ti subissue` failed with \"value not found\" because the parent's children set no longer contained the child.\n\nThe user cannot trust a Sync to be safe unless everything local has already been pushed, which defeats the point of a Git-native tracker.\n\nRoot cause (established): a Deletion today is the *absence* of a ticket's keys. The pull's self-heal for a Stranded index sees a locally deleted ticket as \"a remote ticket missing from the index\" and runs a Reindex, which drops the local serialization ref and fast-forwards the remote tree over the local store. That rewrites every set and list to the remote's contents (tombstoning local-only members, dropping local-only comments) and reinstates the deleted tickets. Operations survive because each is a unique key. The git-meta merge itself is not at fault; the Reindex trigger and the Reindex being destructive are.\n\n\nDeletion becomes an explicit Operation in the ticket's Operation log, so a deleted ticket is never confused with a Stranded index, stays deleted on every clone, and can be undeleted. Reindex becomes additive: it only materializes tickets the index is missing and never rewrites what is already there. A Sync therefore never drops a local operation, comment or link that has not yet been pushed. The model is proven with a TLA+ spec of the sync protocol, enforced by a deterministic regression test reproducing this incident and a property-based two-clone test, and recorded in a new ADR.\n\n\n1. As a maintainer, I want `ti sync` to keep every comment I wrote locally since the last sync, so that my notes are never silently lost.\n2. As a maintainer, I want `ti sync` to keep every sub-issue link and dependency I added locally, so that my ticket map survives syncing.\n3. As a maintainer, I want a ticket I deleted locally to stay deleted after `ti sync`, so that deletions are durable.\n4. As a maintainer, I want a deletion to propagate to other clones on their next Sync, so that everyone sees the same backlog.\n5. As a maintainer, I want `ti delete` to work with no set fan-out writes, so that a deletion is a single atomic operation.\n6. As a maintainer, I want `ti undelete <id>` to bring a deleted ticket back with its history, comments and links intact, so that a mistaken deletion is recoverable.\n7. As a maintainer, I want `ti list` to hide deleted tickets by default, so that the backlog shows only live work.\n8. As a maintainer, I want `ti list --deleted` to show only deleted tickets, so that I can audit or undelete them.\n9. As a maintainer, I want `ti show <deleted-id>` to succeed and display a \"Deleted\" banner with when and by whom, so that I can inspect a deleted ticket.\n10. As a maintainer, I want `ti checkout <deleted-id>` to be refused with a clear message, so that I do not work on a deleted ticket by accident.\n11. As a maintainer, I want `ti next`, `ti stats`, search and the TUI to ignore deleted tickets, so that deleted work never shows up as pending.\n12. As a maintainer, I want a deleted child to be filtered out of its parent's sub-issue list in `ti show`, so that the map reflects live tickets without my having to unlink it.\n13. As a maintainer, I want a Dependency on a deleted ticket to be ignored and shown greyed as \"(deleted)\", so that a deleted blocker never stalls live work.\n14. As a maintainer, I want undeleting a ticket to restore its sub-issue and dependency links automatically, so that undelete is a one-step operation.\n15. As a maintainer, I want a deletion to win over concurrent edits from another clone, so that the deletion outcome is deterministic on every clone.\n16. As a maintainer, I want `ti sync` to warn me when edits arrived for a ticket I had deleted, naming the ticket and how to undelete it, so that nothing lands invisibly.\n17. As an agent, I want that warning in `ti sync --json` as a structured `warnings` array, so that I can act on it programmatically.\n18. As an agent, I want the ticket JSON to carry a `deleted` field (`{at, by}` or `null`), so that I can distinguish deleted tickets without parsing text.\n19. As an agent, I want the published JSON schema extended additively, so that existing consumers keep working.\n20. As a maintainer with an older `ti`, I want `ti list` to keep working in a repo where a newer clone deleted tickets, so that one deletion does not break my clone.\n21. As a maintainer with an older `ti`, I want the release notes and `ti agent` docs to tell me that older binaries show deleted tickets as alive, so that I know to upgrade.\n22. As a maintainer, I want the pull's self-heal to add only the tickets my index is missing, so that a Reindex never touches tickets I already have.\n23. As a maintainer, I want `ti reindex` to be additive by default, so that running it is always safe.\n24. As a maintainer, I want `ti reindex --reset` to refuse when my local store has unsynced changes and tell me how many and what to do, so that a reset never destroys local work by surprise.\n25. As a maintainer, I want `ti reindex --reset --force` available for the case where I truly want the remote to win, so that recovery remains possible.\n26. As a maintainer, I want `ti subissue --clear` and `ti subissue <parent> -t <child>` to succeed even when the parent's children set and the child's parent field disagree, so that I can repair links from the CLI.\n27. As a maintainer, I want `ti depends --remove` to succeed even when the reverse set is already missing the entry, so that link removal is idempotent.\n28. As a maintainer, I want `ti verify` to report parent/children and depends-on/blocks asymmetries, so that link corruption is visible.\n29. As a maintainer, I want a regression test that reproduces this incident end to end against the real binary, so that the bug cannot return.\n30. As a maintainer, I want a property-based test driving two clones through random sequences of creates, comments, links, deletes, undeletes and syncs with injected Race windows, so that the invariants hold under scenarios nobody wrote by hand.\n31. As a maintainer, I want a TLA+ spec of the sync protocol checked with TLC, so that the invariants \"no local operation lost\" and \"no resurrection\" are proven at the design level.\n32. As a maintainer, I want a TLA+ model of the *current* behavior that reproduces this incident as a counterexample trace, so that the ADR's rationale is evidence rather than prose.\n33. As a maintainer, I want an ADR recording that Deletion is an Operation and Reindex is additive, with the model-check result, so that future readers understand why.\n34. As a contributor, I want `CONTEXT.md` to define Deletion, Undeletion, Reindex and Stranded index, so that the team uses one vocabulary.\n35. As a maintainer, I want `ti delete` to keep its existing confirmation behavior, so that the UX does not change for the common path.\n36. As a maintainer, I want a deleted ticket excluded from `ti next` and from open/closed counts in `ti stats`, so that metrics reflect live tickets.\n37. As a maintainer, I want the push's implicit-deletion guard to keep protecting against a Stranded index, so that defense in depth remains.\n\n\n**Deletion is an Operation (ADR 0002 extension, new ADR 0003).**\n- Two new operation kinds: `delete` (optional reason string) and `undelete`. A ticket is deleted iff the highest `(lamport, id)` operation among those two kinds is `delete`. Replay already orders by `(lamport, id)`; no special-casing.\n- Soft deletion: all keys stay in the store and on the remote. The ticket is hidden by projection, not removed. Per-ticket `format-version` stays at 2 because an older `ti` aborts the whole listing on any too-new ticket; older binaries therefore show deleted tickets as alive. Documented in release notes and agent docs.\n- Delete wins over concurrent edits. Edits (operations, comments, set members) that arrive for a deleted ticket are kept on the hidden ticket; undeletion reveals them.\n- Deletion writes no set fan-out: parent `children`, `depends_on`, `blocks` sets are left untouched. Display layers filter deleted ids out of children and dependency lists, and dependency logic treats an edge to a deleted ticket as absent. Undeletion therefore restores links with no extra writes.\n\n**Store API.**\n- `delete_ticket` appends a `delete` op instead of removing keys. New `undelete_ticket` appends `undelete`.\n- `list()` hides deleted tickets by default; a variant lists deleted ones; an \"all ids\" accessor includes them for the pull's missing-id check and for the push deletion guard. Ticket model gains an optional deletion record `{at, by}`.\n- Set removals become idempotent: removing a member that is absent is a success, not an error.\n- `verify` adds an issue kind for link asymmetry (child's parent field vs parent's children set; depends-on vs blocks).\n\n**Sync and Reindex.**\n- The pull's self-heal no longer calls the destructive reindex. It computes the remote ids missing from the index (deleted tickets are indexed, so they never qualify), parses the remote tracking tree, applies only those tickets' keys to the DB additively, then serializes fully so the local ref carries them. The local serialization ref is never deleted.\n- `ti reindex` defaults to the additive path over all remote ids. `--reset` keeps the current drop-ref-and-fast-forward behavior but first serializes the local store fully and compares it to the remote tracking tree; on any difference it refuses with a count of unsynced changes unless `--force`.\n- Sync result gains a `warnings` list; kind `edit-on-deleted` carries the ticket id and count of edits that arrived after the delete op. Text output prints one stderr warning per affected ticket with the undelete hint.\n- The push's implicit-deletion guard (remote ids minus local ids) is unchanged; with soft deletion it now detects only true Stranded indexes.\n\n**CLI.**\n- `ti delete` unchanged in UX, writes the op. New `ti undelete <id>`. `ti list --deleted`. `ti show` renders a Deleted banner; `--json` carries `deleted`. `ti checkout` refuses deleted tickets with non-zero exit. `ti next`, `ti stats`, search, TUI exclude deleted tickets. `ti reindex --reset [--force]`.\n- JSON schema v1 extended additively: `deleted` on the ticket object, `warnings` on the sync result.\n\n**Specification and documentation.**\n- `docs/spec/Sync.tla` + `Sync.cfg`: two clones, one remote, abstract per-ticket operation sets, delete/undelete ops, sets and lists as sets with member tombstones, git-meta merge rules as given (union; removed-on-one-side vs modified-on-other: modified wins), Reindex as \"local gains remote-missing\", push race window. Invariants: `NoLostLocalOp`, `NoResurrection`, `Convergence` after quiescent sync. Not run in CI; a README states the `tla2tools` command.\n- `docs/spec/Sync_v0.tla`: current behavior (key-absence deletion, destructive reindex); TLC must find the 215ee3 trace.\n- ADR 0003 \"Deletion is an operation; reindex is additive\": context, options, consequences, TLC result and state count. ADR 0002's sync-invariant section points to it.\n- `CONTEXT.md` terms already written: Deletion, Undeletion, Reindex, Stranded index; Sync and Operation extended.\n\n**Tracking.** This ticket is the parent. Sub-issues: delete/undelete op + CLI; additive reindex + `--reset` guard; idempotent set removal + verify asymmetry report; regression + property tests; TLA+ specs + ADR 0003. `ti verify --repair` is a separate ticket depending on this one.\n\n\nA good test drives the system through its public surface and asserts on observable results: CLI stdout/stderr/exit/JSON, or `TicketStore` results across real git repos. No assertions on SQLite rows, ref names or tombstone tables.\n\nSeams (highest available, both already exist):\n- **CLI seam**: the `ti` binary in a tempdir git repo with a bare remote, as in the existing integration suite. Used for the deterministic incident regression: sync, then locally add a comment, a sub-issue link, a dependency, and delete a ticket; sync against a remote that holds the previous state; assert comment, link and dependency survive on both clones, the deleted ticket stays deleted everywhere, and the other clone sees the deletion. Also covers `ti undelete`, `--deleted`, the Deleted banner, refused checkout, `reindex --reset` refusal, the sync warning in text and JSON, and idempotent `subissue --clear` on a disagreeing link.\n- **Library seam**: `TicketStore` opened on two sessions sharing a bare remote in a tempdir, as the `test_support` helpers do. Used for the property-based test: `proptest` dev-dependency, random sequences of create/comment/tag/subissue/depends/delete/undelete/sync on either clone, race injection by interposing an edit-and-push on the other clone between a sync's pull and push. Oracle: an in-memory model of expected state. Invariants: every applied operation, comment and link is present on both clones after a quiescent sync; deleted stays deleted; no link asymmetry. Default 32 cases, `PROPTEST_CASES` overrides.\n- Unit tests in the op-log module for delete/undelete ordering and replay (prior art: existing replay tests including unknown-kind skipping).\n\nPrior art: the two-clone race regression tests already in the CLI suite (pre-push hook trick), the sync reporting tests, and the `test_store` library helpers.\n\n\n- Moving comments and sets into the Operation log (ticket 0e7b124).\n- `ti verify --repair` (separate ticket, depends on this one).\n- Hard removal / garbage collection of deleted tickets (`ti gc`).\n- Changing the per-ticket format version or making old binaries hide deleted tickets.\n- Running TLC in CI.\n- Malformed-operation handling in `list()` (currently a hard error).\n- TUI delete/undelete keybindings beyond filtering deleted tickets.\n\n\n- Incident evidence: ticket description and comment on 215ee3; backup ref `refs/ticgit-backup/meta/1794167251-30c9aee0` holds the pre-incident remote state.\n- Order of work: create sub-issues; write `Sync_v0.tla` and confirm TLC reproduces the incident; write `Sync.tla` and check invariants; then implement test-first, CLI regression test red first.\n- Older binaries can still edit a deleted ticket; those edits surface through the `edit-on-deleted` warning on the next sync by an upgraded clone."}}}